-- Heal legacy `scores` rows onto the 0-100 canon.  ⚠ NOT YET RUNNABLE.
--
-- The `.todo` extension is load-bearing. `db/migrate.ts:62` only picks up files
-- ending in `.sql`, so this one is skipped. That is deliberate: the runner
-- applies a file's contents and then UNCONDITIONALLY records it in
-- `_migrations` (migrate.ts:94-96). An all-comments stub would therefore be
-- recorded as "applied" while healing nothing, and would never re-run once the
-- real UPDATEs were written — a silently-recorded no-op is strictly worse than
-- the missing migration this file exists to fix.
--
-- Rename to `035_heal_scores_to_100.sql` once the UPDATEs below are written.
--
-- The 15/07 alpha-feedback log claims this heal shipped as "#252". It never
-- did: the 2026-07-27 audit found no migration and no script anywhere in the
-- repo. What actually protects the founder today is entirely read-side —
-- `to100()` (src/lib/score-display.ts:44) lifts a ≤10 value on the way out, and
-- `baselineScore100` (src/lib/journey/stage-1-idea-validation.ts:45) does the
-- same in the stage check. The write side stopped creating the junk rows
-- (src/lib/artifact-persistence.ts:736 refuses to persist overall 0), but
-- nothing ever repaired what was already there.
--
-- Read-side-only healing means the bad values are still in the table: staging
-- (a SEPARATE Supabase project), any restore, and any consumer that reads
-- `scores` without going through `to100()` all see the raw legacy numbers.
-- This migration makes the repair reproducible instead of a one-off prod query
-- nobody can replay.
--
-- Two legacy shapes, from two different eras of the write path:
--   1. Fabricated zeros — dimension-only artifacts used to INSERT
--      overall_score = 0 rather than NULL, so "not scored yet" is stored as
--      "scored, and it's a 0". Home rendered those as 0/100.
--   2. 0-10 rows — chat score-card/gauge artifacts were prompted with
--      maxScore:10 before the scale was unified, so a 7.2 means 72.
--
-- Must be idempotent: re-running it on already-healed data has to be a no-op,
-- and it has to stay a no-op against rows written by the current 0-100 path.
--
-- ⚠ dev == prod on this project (one Supabase, ghjbxnnkdketrtmebzxl). Run the
--   dry-run block below and read the counts BEFORE applying the UPDATEs.

-- ── Dry run: how many rows does each branch touch? ───────────────────────────
-- Expected on a healthy DB after this has run once: 0 and 0.
--
--   SELECT
--     count(*) FILTER (WHERE overall_score = 0)                          AS junk_zeros,
--     count(*) FILTER (WHERE overall_score > 0 AND overall_score <= 10)  AS legacy_0_10,
--     count(*) FILTER (WHERE overall_score > 10)                         AS already_canon,
--     count(*) FILTER (WHERE overall_score IS NULL)                      AS unscored
--   FROM scores;
--
-- And to eyeball the ambiguous band before committing to it — this is the set
-- the predicate below decides the fate of:
--
--   SELECT project_id, overall_score, dimensions, scored_at
--   FROM scores WHERE overall_score > 0 AND overall_score <= 10
--   ORDER BY scored_at;

-- ── The heal ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- TODO(founder call): write the two UPDATE statements.
--
-- Branch 1 is unambiguous: overall_score = 0 is never a real score under the
-- current canon (the write path refuses to persist it), so it is "unscored"
-- and should become NULL — which is what the read side already assumes.
--
-- Branch 2 is the judgment call, and it is why this is not just `to100()`
-- transcribed into SQL. `overall_score <= 10` cannot distinguish a legacy 0-10
-- score of 8 from a *legitimate* 0-100 score of 8 — and the anti-sycophancy
-- rubric does emit genuinely terrible scores (band ≤40 is "serious warning").
-- ×10-ing one of those silently promotes a failing idea to 80, permanently and
-- invisibly. Three ways to resolve it:
--
--   (a) Accept the collision. Matches to100() exactly, so this introduces no
--       new divergence — reads already behave this way today. Simplest, and
--       consistent, but it makes an existing lossy shortcut permanent.
--   (b) Bound by time. The write-side normalization landed with the 0-100
--       unification (PR #249, 2026-07-21); anything scored after that is canon
--       by construction, so add `AND scored_at < '2026-07-21'`. Narrower and
--       safer, at the cost of a magic date in the migration.
--   (c) Cross-check `dimensions`. A true legacy row has 0-10 dimension values
--       too, so a genuine 0-100 low score is distinguishable when its
--       dimensions are >10 — e.g. require that no dimension value exceeds 10.
--       Most precise, most SQL, and it fails open on rows with null/empty
--       dimensions (which then need a fallback).
--
-- Whichever branch 2 becomes, keep both statements idempotent.
